= Sweet Potato

_Scope: This entry treats sweet potato —_ Ipomoea batatas _(L.) Lam. (Convolvulaceae, the_ morning glory family — not _Solanaceae and not closely related to the common potato_ Solanum tuberosum_). The plant is a_ pre-Columbian American domesticate _(Andean / Mesoamerican / Caribbean origin, with archaeological evidence from ~4000–2500 BCE in coastal Peru), with a remarkable_ pre-Columbian Polynesian transmission _to the Pacific (linguistic and archaeological evidence supports trans-Pacific contact between Polynesians and South Americans before European arrival; the Maori_ kūmara _and Quechua_ kumar _are cognate). Reached China via Manila trade in the late Ming dynasty (~1593), where it became a foundational_ famine-relief staple _under Xu Guangqi's promotion and was eventually entered in_ Bencao Gangmu Shi Yi _(1765). Status: not classical in any Old World canonical tradition (Ayurveda, Tibb, Greco-Roman); post-Ming-classical in Chinese medicine (番薯 fānshǔ); deeply elaborated in the modern_ Okinawan / Japanese satsumaimo _tradition with substantial modern epidemiological-longevity register. Brazilian batata-doce is central to Brazilian and African-diasporic cuisines. Distinct from common potato (Solanum tuberosum, Solanaceae) and from yam (Dioscorea spp.) despite English-language confusion._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* _Ipomoea batatas_ (L.) Lam., family Convolvulaceae. The edible part is the *tuberous root* (botanically a thickened lateral root, *not* a tuber in the strict potato-sense). Hundreds of cultivars with flesh color ranging from white through cream, yellow, orange (carotenoid-rich), pink, deep purple (anthocyanin-rich); skin color similarly varies. *Genus name Ipomoea* from Greek ἴψ (_íps_, "worm") + ὅμοιος (_hómoios_, "like") — for the worm-like twining stem. *Species epithet batatas* — from the Taíno *batata* (the Caribbean indigenous name for sweet potato, the *original* meaning of the word "potato"; the word was applied by 16th-c. European colonists initially to *sweet potato* — encountered first — and only later extended to the Andean *_Solanum tuberosum_*, with the resulting English-language *terminological scramble* preserved to the present). The word *_batata_* → Spanish *batata* / *patata* → Portuguese *batata* → English *potato* (16th c.) is one of the great Atlantic-Columbian linguistic transmissions, and the *original referent of "potato" is the sweet potato*, not the Andean potato.#footnote[Etymology: standard botanical sources; _OED_ (s.v. "potato, n."); Taíno _batata_ → Spanish 16th-c. transmission well-documented in colonial-historical literature. The *priority of sweet potato* as the original "potato" is preserved in the Caribbean Spanish _batata_ usage and is one of the canonical Atlantic-colonial linguistic stories.]

*English:* _Sweet potato_ — distinguishing from (Andean) *potato* (Solanum tuberosum). *Yam* is *not* the same plant — *yam* properly refers to _Dioscorea_ species, but American English (and modern marketing) confusingly uses "yam" for the orange-fleshed sweet potato cultivars. *The "yam vs. sweet potato" confusion in American grocery stores is real and persistent*; the two are botanically unrelated (Dioscoreaceae vs. Convolvulaceae). The Caribbean and Pacific English-speaking diaspora typically maintains the distinction.

*Portuguese (Brazilian):* *Batata-doce* — "sweet potato," distinguishing from *batata* (used for Andean potato in modern Brazilian Portuguese, though the term was originally for sweet potato). Multiple regional Brazilian cultivars: *batata-doce branca, batata-doce amarela, batata-doce-roxa* (purple-fleshed).

*Polynesian (the pre-Columbian Pacific transmission — significant):* *kūmara* (Māori, Cook Islands Māori, Tahitian); *ʻuala* (Hawaiian); *kumara* / *kumala* — the word is cognate across Polynesian languages and is *linguistically related to the Quechua kumar / k'umar* (the Andean coastal indigenous name) — *evidence of pre-Columbian Polynesian-Andean contact*. The *Polynesian pre-Columbian sweet potato* is one of the most compelling archaeological-linguistic cases for pre-Columbian Pacific trans-oceanic contact. Modern Easter Island and Polynesian agricultural history is centrally about kūmara cultivation.#footnote[Pre-Columbian Polynesian sweet potato: extensive ethnobotanical and linguistic literature; the Quechua _kumar_ / Polynesian _kūmara_ cognate is well-attested; archaeological dating of sweet potato in Polynesia (Cook Islands, Easter Island, New Zealand) pre-dates European contact; the contact hypothesis is now widely accepted (Roullier et al., _PNAS_ 2013; Montenegro et al.; Storey et al. on the parallel Polynesian chicken in Chile pre-contact). One of the great pre-Columbian Pacific-American contact cases.]

*Chinese:* *番薯 (fānshǔ)*, "foreign-tuber" — the canonical Chinese name; the *foreign-origin marker 番 fān* (used also for 番茄 fānqié tomato, 番椒 fānjiāo chili pepper) explicitly marks the post-Columbian / Ming-era American introduction. *Other names:* 甘薯 (gān shǔ) "sweet tuber"; 紅薯 (hóng shǔ) "red tuber"; 地瓜 (dì guā) "earth-melon" — the regional northern Chinese folk name; 山芋 (shān yù) — Jiangsu-Zhejiang regional name. The introduction is well-documented historically: sweet potato reached *Fujian* in *1593* via *Chen Zhenlong* — a Chinese trader who smuggled cuttings from Spanish Manila past Spanish trade prohibitions; *Xu Guangqi* (徐光啟) — the late-Ming polymath and Christian convert — promoted sweet potato cultivation as *famine-relief* across China during the 1608 Jiangsu famine, writing the *_Treatise on the Sweet Potato_ (_Gānshǔ shū_ 甘薯疏, 1608)*. The plant became *foundational to Qing-dynasty population growth*, providing reliable calories on marginal land; modern Chinese famine-prevention agricultural history credits the sweet potato (alongside maize and peanut) as enabling the doubling of Chinese population in the 17th–19th centuries.#footnote[Chinese introduction history: standard Ming-Qing economic and agricultural history (e.g. Mazumdar, _Sugar and Society in China_; Ho Ping-ti, _Studies on the Population of China_); Xu Guangqi, _Gānshǔ shū_ (1608); modern Chinese agricultural-history scholarship. The Chen Zhenlong / Manila origin story (1593) is preserved in Fujian local-history literature.]

*Japanese:* *サツマイモ (satsumaimo)*, "Satsuma potato" — for the Satsuma domain (modern Kagoshima Prefecture, southern Kyushu) where the plant was introduced from Ryukyu (and ultimately China via the same trans-Pacific route) in the 17th c. and became a regional staple. Modern Japanese cultivation is centered in Kagoshima, Chiba, Ibaraki. The Japanese cultivar *Beni Azuma* (beni = red, azuma = east) and *Naruto Kintoki* are famous regional varieties. *Yaki imo* (焼き芋, baked sweet potato) is a *winter street-food canonical* in Japan — the truck-vendor selling roasted yaki imo with the characteristic vocal call is preserved as a winter scene.

*Sanskrit:* *No classical Sanskrit name* — the plant is post-Columbian to South Asia. Modern Hindi *शकरकंद (shakarkand)*, *रतालू (ratālū — sometimes, with overlap to yam)* — modern coinages. *No classical Ayurvedic entry; reasoned extension only*, flagged.

*Arabic:* *البطاطا الحلوة (al-baṭāṭā al-ḥulwa)* — "sweet potato"; modern transliteration / coinage. *Not classical in Tibb*; no Avicennan, Ibn al-Bayṭār, or Tibb al-Nabawī attestation.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View — Post-Ming Classical

*番薯 / 甘薯 (fānshǔ / gānshǔ)* is *post-classical in the strict Shennong-Bencao-Gangmu sense* (sweet potato was unknown to Li Shizhen in 1578, before the 1593 introduction) but enters Chinese pharmacopoeial literature in the *Qing dynasty* — appearing in *Bencao Gangmu Shi Yi* (本草綱目拾遺, "Bencao Gangmu Supplement" by Zhao Xuemin, 1765) and in subsequent late-Qing and modern Chinese-medicine literature. The herb's clinical status is *food-grade staple* rather than a pharmacy drug, but the classical dietetic register is developed.

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Neutral (平) to slightly warm (微溫).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Kidney (腎).

*Actions & indications:*
- *補中和血 (bǔ zhōng hé xuè)* — tonifies the middle and harmonizes blood.
- *益氣生津 (yì qì shēng jīn)* — augments qi and generates fluids — for thirst, dryness, weakness.
- *寬腸通便 (kuān cháng tōng biàn)* — eases the intestines and promotes bowel movement — for constipation (the fiber and starch register).
- *Dietetic use:* sweet potato congee (番薯粥), baked sweet potato (烤番薯), sweet potato leaves as green vegetable (the leaves are an underappreciated nutritious green) — daily-tonic preparations in modern Chinese dietetics.

*Cautions:* damp-heat patterns; food stagnation; the dietary register is constitutionally neutral but heavy use can be damp-generating in some constitutions.#footnote[Sweet potato in Chinese pharmacopoeial literature: Zhao Xuemin, _Bencao Gangmu Shi Yi_ (本草綱目拾遺, 1765); modern Chinese dietetic literature (e.g. Kastner, _Chinese Nutrition Therapy_). The reading is post-classical but well-elaborated in modern Chinese dietetics.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Sweet potato is not classical in Ayurveda._ The reading by extension would place it in the *madhura-snigdha-guru* register: pacifies Vāta and Pitta; mildly aggravates Kapha; uṣṇa vīrya (slightly heating); madhura vipāka; karma _balya, bṛṃhaṇa_. Modern Indian Ayurvedic dietetic literature treats sweet potato as a *post-classical sweet root staple* — *post-classical extension only*, flagged.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Sweet potato is not classical in Tibb._ The plant is post-Columbian and post-Avicennan; no canonical _adwiya mufrada_ entry. Modern Unani may include it as a post-classical sweet-nutritive food; placement on the humoral axis by reasoned extension: Hot 1° / Moist 1° to 2° (similar to carrot and other sweet starchy roots). *Flagged as post-classical extension.*

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫) — post-classical dietetic

- *番薯粥 / 紅薯粥 (Sweet potato congee)* — daily dietetic staple in much of central and southern China; *the canonical winter Spleen-Stomach-supporting porridge*.
- *烤番薯 (yaki imo / baked sweet potato)* — Beijing winter street food, equivalent to the Japanese yaki imo register.
- *甘薯藤 / 番薯葉 (sweet potato leaves)* — eaten as green vegetable, especially in Taiwanese and southern Chinese cuisine; *more nutritious than spinach* by some metrics (calcium, iron, β-carotene).
- *Compound dietetic preparations:* sweet potato with red beans, with millet, with ginger.

=== Japanese (the satsumaimo register)

- *Yaki imo (焼き芋)* — the *canonical winter street food* — sweet potato roasted in stones in a vendor's truck-oven; preserved continuously through the modern Japanese winter; the *truck-vendor's vocal call* is a recognized Japanese winter cultural sound.
- *Daigaku imo (大学芋, "university sweet potato")* — sweet potato deep-fried with sugar-and-soy glaze and sesame; an Edo / Meiji-era university-student staple.
- *Tempura* — sweet potato tempura is one of the canonical vegetable tempuras.
- *Shochu (焼酎)* — Kagoshima sweet-potato shochu (imo-jōchū, 芋焼酎) is the canonical Kyushu distilled spirit; uniquely Japanese among major spirit traditions.
- *Sweet potato as Okinawan staple* — central in the famous *Okinawan centenarian dietary pattern*, particularly the purple-fleshed *beni imo* (紅芋), with substantial anthocyanin content and *significant modern epidemiological-longevity register* (the Okinawa Centenarian Study; the herb's role in the world's most-studied longevity diet is among the more striking modern food-and-longevity findings).

=== Western / Greco-Roman and Ayurveda / Tibb

No classical Western tradition (the plant is post-Columbian); no classical Ayurvedic or Tibb tradition. Modern Western: sweet potato is a globally distributed staple with extensive culinary register; modern Western nutrition treats it as a nutrient-dense whole food with favorable glycemic and antioxidant profile.

=== Brazilian / African-diasporic

- *Batata-doce assada* — baked sweet potato; canonical Brazilian comfort food and breakfast staple.
- *Batata-doce frita* — fried; restaurant register.
- *Pudim de batata-doce* — sweet potato pudding (Brazilian and Portuguese rural register).
- *African-diasporic uses* — sweet potato is central in West African (Igbo, Yoruba) and African-American Southern cooking; Southern US *candied yams* (which are sweet potatoes) at Thanksgiving; African-Brazilian / African-Caribbean use is extensive.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Sweet potato is a *warm-tropical-to-subtropical* annual / tender perennial — adapted to *warm growing seasons (frost-free, 4–6 months minimum), moderate rainfall, well-drained soils*. The plant tolerates considerable variability and is cultivated from the tropics (Africa, Caribbean, southern Asia, Brazil) through subtropical regions (southern China, southern Japan, Mediterranean) to *warm-temperate climates with adequate summer warmth* (southeastern US, central China). The hardiness and reliable productivity made sweet potato the *foundational famine-relief staple* in 17th–19th-century China and a *globally distributed food-security crop* in modern agricultural economy. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Hot-humid tropical and subtropical (the canonical growing climate)

The native and primary cultivation climate. *Sweet potato as staple* — daily dietary use; Caribbean / sub-Saharan African / South Asian / South Chinese / Brazilian register.

*Cares.* The sweet-warming-snigdha profile is constitutionally apt for tropical climates as a basic carbohydrate staple; modern Pitta-loaded Indian use would moderate heavy daily intake in the hottest months. The Okinawan tradition uses purple beni imo with substantial anthocyanin antioxidant load — a *climatically-calibrated longevity register*.

=== Continental seasonal-extreme (China, Korea, Japan, central US)

The cool-winter / warm-summer pattern; sweet potato is *winter-stored* as a major calorie source; the *winter yaki imo register* in Japan and China is climatically calibrated for cold-winter use.

=== Cool-damp Atlantic / Mediterranean

Modern cultivation in southern France, Italy, Portugal, southern Spain has expanded; the constitutional reading places sweet potato as a *warming-snigdha cool-season food* — climatically apt for cool-damp constitutional pressure.

=== Cold-dry continental / steppe and polar

Sweet potato is *not classical* in these climates; the plant cannot grow in cold-short-summer conditions. Modern import is post-classical.

=== Modern transposition

Modern globalized health-food register has spread sweet potato across all climates; the carotenoid (orange-fleshed) and anthocyanin (purple-fleshed) registers are widely consumed for nutritional benefit. The classical Chinese / Japanese constitutional register provides the most-elaborated traditional guidance. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _viruddha_ framework.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Approximate composition (raw sweet potato, orange-fleshed, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~86 kcal; Water ~77 g
- Carbohydrate ~20 g (starch predominant, some sugars); fiber ~3 g
- Protein ~1.6 g; Fat ~0.1 g
- *Minerals:* potassium ~337 mg, magnesium ~25 mg, calcium ~30 mg, iron ~0.6 mg, manganese, copper
- *Vitamins:* *vitamin A — very high* (~14,000 IU as β-carotene in orange-fleshed cultivars — *one of the highest plant sources*); vitamin C; vitamin B6; folate
- *Carotenoids:* β-carotene (orange-fleshed); lutein and zeaxanthin (eye-health register)
- *Anthocyanins:* purple-fleshed cultivars are very high in anthocyanins (peonidin and cyanidin derivatives) — comparable to blueberries by anthocyanin content; the Okinawan beni imo / Hawaiian molokai purple cultivars are *significant anthocyanin sources*
- *Phenolic compounds:* chlorogenic acid, ferulic acid; antioxidant register
- *Glycemic index:* moderate (~50–60 baked); *significantly lower than potato* — favorable diabetic-dietary register
- *Resistant starch* — modest; supports prebiotic / gut-health register#footnote[Composition data: USDA FoodData Central; standard nutritional databases; recent reviews in _Food Chemistry_, _Journal of Functional Foods_; carotenoid and anthocyanin chemistry well-characterized.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Vitamin A precursor* — orange-fleshed sweet potato is *one of the most important global sources of provitamin A*; biofortified orange-fleshed cultivars (HarvestPlus) have substantially reduced vitamin A deficiency in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia in the 21st c., one of the success stories of modern nutritional intervention.
- *Glycemic control* — moderate-low GI; supports diabetic dietary management; multiple clinical studies support glycemic-favorable profile.
- *Antioxidant* — substantial; anthocyanin-rich purple cultivars particularly.
- *Cardiovascular protective (epidemiologic)* — daily sweet potato intake in Okinawan and other longevity-cohort populations is associated with favorable cardiovascular outcomes.
- *Anti-inflammatory* — modest preclinical.
- *Anti-cancer (preclinical)* — anthocyanins from purple sweet potato show preclinical anti-cancer activity; clinical translation preliminary.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary register:* unrestricted at normal dietary intake.
- *Carotenoid considerations:* very heavy daily intake (multiple servings per day for weeks) can cause *carotenemia* — yellowing of skin from excess β-carotene — harmless and reversible, but worth noting.
- *Pregnancy:* safe; the high vitamin A content from carotenoids (as opposed to retinol) is *not teratogenic* (the body regulates carotenoid → retinol conversion), unlike high-dose retinol supplementation.
- *Diabetes:* lower-GI than potato, favorable for glycemic management.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges from standard nutrition literature; the carotenemia and pregnancy considerations are standard nutritional toxicology. Okinawan dietary literature: Willcox et al., _Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences_; the Okinawa Centenarian Study and related publications.]

